meta {
  name: 2 Appointments (ownership-guarded)
  type: http
  seq: 2
}
get {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/calendar/appointments/{{providerEmail}}
}
tests {
  test("200 for the owner, 403 for anyone else", function () {
    expect([200, 403, 404]).to.include(res.getStatus());
  });
}
docs {
  Same fix as `1 Availability`, and the more sensitive of the two: this returns the provider's full
  appointment list, **each entry carrying a customer email**. Any authenticated user could read any
  provider's client book before F-016.

  ⚠️ **Design invariant worth knowing if you ever edit `Calendar/Program.cs`:** the ownership guard must
  stay **above** the cache read. Both routes cache under a key derived from `{email}` — the request
  *subject*, never the *caller* — so ordering is the only thing preventing a cross-tenant leak.
  `CalendarOwnershipTest.T006_AWarmCacheIsNotServedToADifferentPrincipal` fails if it is ever reordered.

  A `404` where you expected data can be a `CacheAside` lock timeout rather than missing data —
  `CacheAside` returns `default!` after 500 ms and has no test of its own.
}
